8599-260c-5867-88ec-692aa4d19c71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089795 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089795 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089795 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089795 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089795 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089795 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089795 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089795 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186089797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089795 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "88da4f35-85b0-53f0-a65f-db9edf474b77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089796 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089796 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089796 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9d76cebc-b6fb-5524-8283-b87a8706d0a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089797 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089797 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089797 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a035904c-4fe1-5a9f-a9bf-e49750aa30b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089798 #edb/ref 1080 "None" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089798 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089798 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f97d5917-25f1-5529-9b0f-beee8799d9a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089799 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089799 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089799 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "48009762-6e64-51c1-8c34-e8d675fca580" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089800 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089800 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089800 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "678c699c-69d3-50d3-949b-5005fc07d7cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091944 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091944 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/45/45a423930fb07c365f10cdfbdeb3d45ef337078fa77e1b0699388088438d4c70.png)\n\n \n\nWhat is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{ 1,2,3,4,5 \\}$ whose indicator function $\\boldsymbol{1}_A(x)$ is shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091944 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2acf7f4e-1d5d-56e5-8478-9611a3afde73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091944 #edb/ref 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n![](images/15/159dcc0eb98804f6d4d281603de45cf00922023ce1d427ff73a7188bea38e40d.svg)\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $1,2,$ and $5$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{ 1,2,5 \\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091945 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091945 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\mathbf{1}_{A}(-1) = 0, \\qquad \\mathbf{1}_{B}(-1) = 1\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 0$\n\nIII. $-1 \\in A \\cup B$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091945 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc45cc10-61e3-5cb7-babc-7253a5158c4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091945 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overline{A},$ $A \\cap B,$ and $A \\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(-1) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(-1),{1}_{B}(-1)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{0,1\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1,$ we conclude that $-1$ belongs to $A \\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Indicator Function of a Set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186091944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2e380013-0002-5a17-bbf7-e9ce89e6331d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1039 "Using Properties of Indicator Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186091945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1045 "Indicator Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186093249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "32f8f5ef-15f3-5904-a759-74a6bc8770e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092600 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092600 #edb/ref 1069 "q-172706" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092600 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092600 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b2/b2919328788c6762d1a0b56225b95749e88973ec84803584be0d336f63033566.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}$ whose indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ is shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092600 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c3c4858f-5a41-55b3-bb89-5356156d99e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092600 #edb/ref 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $a,b,$ and $d$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{a,b,d\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092604 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092604 #edb/ref 1069 "q-172723" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092604 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092604 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{1}_{A}(5)=1,\\,{1}_{B}(5)=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5)=0$\n\nIII. $5\\in A\\cup B$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092604 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bab9e63e-a883-5b00-876f-108808a6cc8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092604 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(5),{1}_{B}(5)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{1,0\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5)=1,$ we conclude that $5$ belongs to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093249 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093249 #edb/ref 1061 46177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093249 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a653fede-ccbc-5f2f-b061-934db5fa0d21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093249 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093250 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093250 #edb/ref 1061 46178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093250 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7390f8dd-a510-5070-8b26-59db8798d82a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093250 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093251 #edb/ref 1061 46181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093251 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d6d114da-0075-5e74-9dfb-15ec23ed1bd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093251 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093252 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093252 #edb/ref 1061 46185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093252 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d765dcbb-6e69-5616-b71c-25e2c514aa3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093252 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093495 #edb/ref 1064 "An **indicator function** is a function that maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset.\n\nThe indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n![](images/af/af268dd04b20dde1e7216e63839f309f572615b59f57f81515cd2e7998c1f45d.svg)\n\nFor example, let $A = \\{ a,c \\}$ be a subset of the universal set $X=\\{ a,b,c,d,e \\}.$ Then, the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_A(x)$ of $A$ is the following:\n\n![](images/7b/7b1ec0a80d5ec7dc9974d2ebaf6984e94faa00d73ec624dc48d56addbe701ee1.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the elements $a$ and $c$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1,$ and all other elements are mapped to $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093495 #edb/ref 1065 8006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093495 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "20986866-6719-520f-b707-cf6b26e3823b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093495 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093496 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n![](images/74/7408ad6ce2700ac59d8e6a78c511ba2b51551e6b9d039ee7e2f8548f4177802e.svg)\n\nTherefore, the indicator function of the complement $\\overline{A}$ is given by \n\n![](images/de/de1a51c00e16d836fde350e0936fb9cb63124c72b49174210be3e6f6faa152da.svg)\n\nThis means that the indicator function of $A$ and its complement are related as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the indicator functions of $A \\cap B$ and union $A \\cup B$ can be expressed through the indicator functions of $A$ and $B.$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *intersection*, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *union*, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)+{1}_{B}(x)-{1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can prove these results by splitting them into cases." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093496 #edb/ref 1065 8007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093496 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a111c445-39e3-5ddd-8272-48ef3795a3b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093496 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of Indicator Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186409880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409880 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409880 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409880 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409880 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "21f37bd0-557b-529a-88f6-615287ca253d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409884 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409884 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409884 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409884 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409884 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409884 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409884 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409884 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409884 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "72880716-881b-572f-a482-436f0d9e99ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409887 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409887 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409887 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409887 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409887 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409887 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409887 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409887 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409887 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d41cebf3-62a2-5784-a1f4-13fc481b665c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409888 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454537 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186409888 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454538 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186409888 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454539 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186409888 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174385" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409888 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409888 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${1}_{A}(q)=0,$ which of the following statements about $\\overset{―}{A}$ are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q)=1$\n\n- **II.** ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q)=0$\n\n- **III.** $q\\in \\overset{―}{A}$\n\n*Hint: ${1}_{A}$ denotes the indicator function of the set $A$ and ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}$ denotes the indicator function of the complement of $A.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186409888 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09ca95e9-e7fd-5d14-80c3-ab64bc50a23d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409888 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q) & =1-{1}_{A}(q) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q)=1,$ we conclude that $q$ belongs to $\\overset{―}{A}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409889 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409889 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174363" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409889 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409889 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ae/aeb0c87a33cf30494636f0a8f8d411033ba064385ad04065abff184752ea99d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{-4,-3,-2,-1,0\\}$ whose indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ is shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409889 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3574e15c-1d81-5d76-a394-37484fa9dfbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409889 #edb/ref 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $-4$ and $0$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{-4,0\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409892 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409892 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409892 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409892 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7c/7c60072882a6effae61da543bfddff1053ab0bcc924d12c49f5acd697b0871c4.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}$ whose indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ is shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409892 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a66f5e5d-805e-5011-9534-19a9e92d3193" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409892 #edb/ref 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $b,c$ and $e$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{b,c,e\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409893 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454534 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186409893 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454535 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186409893 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454536 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186409893 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174380" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409893 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409893 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${1}_{A}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements about $\\overset{―}{A}$ are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(0)=1$\n\n- **II.** ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(0)=0$\n\n- **III.** $0\\in \\overset{―}{A}$\n\n*Hint: ${1}_{A}$ denotes the indicator function of the set $A$ and ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}$ denotes the indicator function of the complement of $A.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186409893 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8a05322-998a-5d01-8b89-68a8bc93dd0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409893 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\