86502079 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174396" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502079 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502079 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{1}_{A}(r)=0,\\,{1}_{B}(r)=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r)=0$\n\nIII. $r\\in A\\cap B$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502079 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "221288b9-d358-51b3-a7a7-1f68c1f5fbe9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502079 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(r),{1}_{B}(r)\\} \\\\  & =min\\{0,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r)=0,$ we conclude that $r$ does not belong to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502090 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502090 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174358" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502090 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502090 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/bb/bbd8b82211b2b3a19935aae010e15083ce3553984473444ae6f7a720b682d7f5.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{p,q,r,s,t\\}$ whose indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ is shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502090 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33d9e7d3-a57a-5882-990d-c4b03eec3609" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502090 #edb/ref 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $q$ and $t$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{q,t\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
